1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,638
Part four.

2
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:02,500
Twenty-five agents on his phone.

3
00:00:02,925 --> 00:00:04,775
Here's the contradiction that isn't one.

4
00:00:05,188 --> 00:00:07,837
Cuban calls himself the biggest proponent of AI

5
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:08,388
there is.

6
00:00:08,812 --> 00:00:10,338
He's been in tech for decades,

7
00:00:10,638 --> 00:00:12,962
and he says this dwarfs everything before it.

8
00:00:13,388 --> 00:00:15,962
He runs about twenty-five AI agents that handle

9
00:00:15,975 --> 00:00:17,100
the tedious work for him.

10
00:00:17,525 --> 00:00:18,162
Because of them,

11
00:00:18,538 --> 00:00:21,062
he says, he finally read a non-fiction book

12
00:00:21,075 --> 00:00:23,075
for the first time in twenty-five years.

13
00:00:23,488 --> 00:00:24,312
One real example.

14
00:00:24,725 --> 00:00:26,000
At Cost Plus Drugs,

15
00:00:26,188 --> 00:00:28,538
his company that sells cheaper prescription medicine,

16
00:00:28,888 --> 00:00:30,888
he's fighting what he calls the big healthcare

17
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:31,675
conglomerates.

18
00:00:32,088 --> 00:00:34,062
Now an agent tells him every time one

19
00:00:34,075 --> 00:00:34,988
of them gets sued,

20
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:37,112
and every time they bid on a contract.

21
00:00:37,525 --> 00:00:39,938
It even drafts the public records requests to

22
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:40,688
get copies.

23
00:00:41,112 --> 00:00:41,550
Before,

24
00:00:41,925 --> 00:00:42,863
he'd have had to hire,

25
00:00:43,138 --> 00:00:44,238
train and manage someone.

26
00:00:44,662 --> 00:00:45,550
So it never got done.

27
00:00:45,975 --> 00:00:47,788
Bartlett catches the obvious problem.

28
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,900
Aren't those tasks someone would have been paid

29
00:00:49,912 --> 00:00:50,250
to do?

30
00:00:50,662 --> 00:00:51,438
Cuban admits it.

31
00:00:51,862 --> 00:00:53,387
He might once have hired an intern at

32
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,300
fifteen or twenty dollars an hour.

33
00:00:55,712 --> 00:00:57,812
His argument is that the time saved creates

34
00:00:57,825 --> 00:01:00,800
new ideas, and new businesses that hire different

35
00:01:00,812 --> 00:01:01,188
people.

36
00:01:01,625 --> 00:01:03,038
He calls it creative destruction.

37
00:01:03,462 --> 00:01:06,050
He went to college near Terre Haute, Indiana,

38
00:01:06,362 --> 00:01:08,275
where RCA once made records,

39
00:01:08,525 --> 00:01:10,675
then CDs, then DVDs.

40
00:01:10,888 --> 00:01:11,675
Cassettes are gone.

41
00:01:12,088 --> 00:01:13,200
Money in music isn't.

42
00:01:13,625 --> 00:01:15,012
But he's honest that the path will be

43
00:01:15,025 --> 00:01:16,413
painful for a lot of people.

44
00:01:16,825 --> 00:01:18,900
His advice to his own kids is blunt.

45
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:20,000
Learn agents.

46
00:01:20,412 --> 00:01:22,638
Anyone who does will be far more productive

47
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:23,962
than anyone who doesn't.

48
00:01:24,375 --> 00:01:26,162
And the edge isn't the information.

49
00:01:26,575 --> 00:01:28,325
It's knowing which questions to ask,

50
00:01:28,612 --> 00:01:30,150
and judging four different answers.

51
00:01:30,575 --> 00:01:32,400
Which brings us to how Cuban built his

52
00:01:32,412 --> 00:01:33,000
own edge.
